#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "58d92951-d993-50ec-806c-802c627ada96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5213" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/7a5d03c5fc0581217a488b76e6d39486.png)" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4x - 6y &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3 \\cdot 0^{2} - 4x - 6 \\cdot 0 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 2 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 4x + 4) - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 6 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± \\sqrt{6} \\\\\nx &= 2 ± \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a7fe6697-669c-57e2-8415-4bd40cf76dac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5214" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((0 - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/c435c838bf3a88b169499c58834c75c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "85e115e3-4f55-53e6-9d3b-decb3dc02267" :question/math-academy-id "e-5220" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((0 - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 12 \\\\\ny - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{12} \\\\\ny - 3 &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ny &= 3 ± 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/686d5b60b8ea3f16af57af3ed5ef341d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "5cbcffa6-eb3d-5438-ba08-4b47cbbf76ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-5221" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $5x^2 + 2y^2 - 20x - 8y + 13 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20x - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20 \\cdot 0 - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2y^{2} - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y^{2} - 4y) + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[y^{2} - 4y + 4 - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y - 2)^{2} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\n, there is no $y$ such that\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/dadec72b5f61bde4c28bdf1079eb4729.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf660b4-d4f5-5e3c-bbc1-0920eaadb2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267862} {:answer/id #uuid "7538da33-4499-5a56-8ee1-78740b96168b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267863} {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a01ae-c095-5d05-8219-c89f6c6f83db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267865} {:answer/id #uuid "8d804631-6000-5274-9589-897a4691f407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d0653b-a7e0-51d3-b91c-7d5c19f29317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ad77f8-16b1-5bb8-ad29-59b9ef0fc86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267869} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb48277-c593-5ee0-8b65-8b6011b2eb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267870} {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b23ee3-7a6f-51f4-98c2-c0dccec79917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267872} {:answer/id #uuid "26a1c010-0e1b-5e5b-bc36-75a8df257575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32eca97-6dd3-5ee1-a315-2b1fcbdb1bb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50559ddf-048e-5af7-bb48-9f6c1a546726" :question/math-academy-id "q-16646" :question/problem "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b7ca58-6523-5b4a-89aa-333c3f91d9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267876} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d2ee77-b7e7-5264-8fa6-a323a3d65daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267877} {:answer/id #uuid "e283c48b-6c89-599d-b881-478cdb455ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267878} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} {:answer/id #uuid "d8635ba2-c02d-5b70-adf7-4d27026af194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d96e1d-dc55-5d53-92ab-ea6ad3148922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c2f5cb-5a68-5c5e-91e4-a196dc268883" :question/math-academy-id "q-16647" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e89b1e-0fa0-5146-819c-88d513ab13c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935} :answer-field/id #uuid "782fc26c-27bb-5025-9bdd-006f8586f5cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "167fbd42-673a-5411-ba0f-804405134964" :question/math-academy-id "q-327146" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2c761-77e4-540e-b74a-7c7343e36a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945} {:answer/id #uuid "4c01566b-b810-5053-86db-734c63ece004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267946} {:answer/id #uuid "e967b97f-698b-5bc0-9412-c86232ffb994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267947} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa028fc-f755-56af-8dd4-05d189f371f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a00b5-b64e-53d2-b2a0-a158b06a42ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64b3860f-fd90-5411-a2e5-411bcddf0dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72914" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c0df-3832-577b-827a-26d8e4d65bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6f6ab-b141-5abe-bc31-f1709de323bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3, 0)$ and $(5, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087320} {:answer/id #uuid "0481e7c3-6950-5fe0-8d3d-2ea93680d586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087321} {:answer/id #uuid "398d82c9-4cc1-547d-9f52-d8b2e613f1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087322} {:answer/id #uuid "53af3dde-ce2b-5aee-a79c-d7d102c042de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 0)$ and $(4, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c0df-3832-577b-827a-26d8e4d65bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6b1db7-08c4-56d0-a6bb-ce1468649e28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fc78834-5b61-5384-b707-af7562e6a703" :question/math-academy-id "q-9457" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $((x + 4)^{2})/(3) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(6) = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)"}]}
